  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1225 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nThe potential for rain showers and thunderstorms will increase on \nFriday, especially during the afternoon into the evening time frame. \nAnother shortwave will move southeast from the Northern High Plains \ntowards the Midwest during the day. This wave will usher in a cold \nfront which will move over the forecast area on Friday. Given the \nlow confidence in any showers during the morning, plenty of \ninstability should be able to build during the day as temperatures \nrise into the 80s and dew points quickly climb. Forecast soundings \ncontinue to depict MLCAPE values rising up to 3000 J/kg out ahead of \nthe front tomorrow afternoon. Deep layer shear is not all that \nimpressive out ahead of this system, with model guidance showing \nvalues around 30 kts, but they are sufficient enough to at least \nattempt to build organized convection along the front. Any storms \nthat are able to form in this environment will have the potential to \nbecome strong to severe, with the most likely hazard being damaging \nwind gusts as a linear complex may develop along the front. If \nany storms can remain discrete, some large hail cannot be ruled \nout. Storms should be relatively quick moving along the front \nand should be out of the area by late evening.\n\nAfter that system departs to the east, the region looks to be \nunder mainly northwest flow for much of the remainder of this \nforecast period. This is in part to troughing over the \nnortheastern US with a ridge of high pressure building over the \nRockies into the Great Plains. Very mild conditions are likely \nthis weekend through the start of the next work week with \nafternoon highs in the 70s to low 80s. Upper level high pressure\nover the southern High Plains may retreat back west during the \nmiddle of next week which would increase the potential for \nmoisture return over the Midwest. However, that is still nearly \na week out so any slight change in the pattern could occur so \nconfidence is low at this time.",
